    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-12726",
      "problem": "Find the truth set of $P(x) \\lor Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $3x -1 < 5$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq 3$",
      "worked_solution": "The truth set of $P(x) \\lor Q(x)$ represents the *union* of the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & <5 \\\\ 3x & <6 \\\\ x & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is\n$T_P = (-\\infty,2).$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n$T_Q = [3,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∨Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,2)\\cup [3,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253347/observations/step-e12726-ce71e166048c4648fa54.html",
